WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.080
Good morning Hank its Tuesday.

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:02.160
So today I want to

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:05.820
discuss what we know about the relationship between Donald Trump's Presidential Campaign

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:10.240
and the Russian government's interference in our 2016 Elections, but first a couple notes.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:16.600
Russia is a huge and massively diverse country of 144 million people but in the context of this Election meddling,

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:19.540
we are talking specifically about Vladimir Putin and his government

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:22.040
Russia and the US are currently adversaries.

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:25.920
Putin's regime has sought to undermine democracy and democratic institutions around the world

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.520
and in the US's view invaded a sovereign nation, but whether the United States and Russia are friends

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:34.620
is not actually that relevant to the legal questions at hand.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.880
Also there is a lot of speculation about this story online for the purposes of this video

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:42.060
I'm only going to refer to events that have either been confirmed by the people

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:47.040
who participated in them or reported independently by two separate news organizations.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:49.620
Sources as always in the video info below.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:50.120
Okay here we go...

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:53.260
The Department of Homeland Security and the Office of the Director of National Intelligence

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:55.940
have stated with high confidence that the Russian government

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:01.500
sought to influence the outcome of the US Presidential election favoring Donald Trump over Hillary Clinton

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:03.680
They have also stated that Russia's leader, Vladimir Putin,

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:08.360
personally oversaw this operation, which actually shouldn't come as that much of a surprise.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:10.860
The Putin regime has a history of trying to sway foreign elections

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:15.840
and, also, Trump's policy positions were broadly much more favorable to Putin than Clinton's

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:16.900
policy positions.

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:20.780
I mean, as far back as 2007 Trump said that Putin was "doing a great job".

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:25.660
The Trump campaign even cut proposed language from the official republican party platform about supporting Ukraine

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:27.840
and their ongoing conflict with Russia.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.000
Okay so the Putin regime meddled in our election in at least three ways:

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:35.320
[1] via a disinformation campaign in which they created and shared inaccurate news stories

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:37.780
[2] by trying to breach election data in several states, and

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:42.460
[3] by hacking the Democratic National Committee and DNC leader John  Podesta to access their emails

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:44.140
which they then released to Wikileaks.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:48.700
Now you may not believe all of that, and certainly both Wikileaks and the Russian government deny it, but there is

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:55.760
overwhelming agreement not just within US intelligence circles but also among both Republican and Democratic members of congress

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:59.720
who, as you may have noticed, struggle to find common ground on pretty much anything.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:04.800
The email hack in particular made a significant impact because it created a huge imbalance of information.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:09.320
The public had access to all these democratic emails, many of which one might charitably describe as

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:09.820
sleazy,

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:13.100
but not to any similar Republican emails that might have existed

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:16.880
It's a bit like saying: "Can you believe all this crap in candidate X's tax returns,"

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:20.100
when candidate Y doesn't release their tax returns at all.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:24.500
Now you can say, and you ought to, that political parties just shouldn't be sleazy.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:27.280
But my point is only that it created 
an information imbalance, and

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:31.400
that, by choosing whose emails we saw,
Putin had a lot of say in the election narrative.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:34.000
Of course none of that means the election was illegitimate -

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:34.500
it wasn't -

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:36.000
or that Trump didn't win -

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:36.700
he did.

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:40.300
And benefitting from a foreign government's propaganda initiative is not a crime.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:43.400
However, colluding with that government *is*.

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:44.100
Probably.

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:48.500
US election law says that a foreign national, 
be they Canadian or Russian or Martian,

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:54.400
cannot make a contribution or a donation of any 
thing of value in connection with a US election.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:03:02.000
It also says that no American can solicit, 
or knowingly accept, 
a donation or any thing of value from a foreign national.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:05.500
I suspect we're going to hear the phrase "thing of value" a lot in the coming months, by the way,

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:09.700
in part because the courts haven't been super clear at deciding what it means.

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:12.920
If, hypothetically, you received damning information about your opponent

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:15.780
from a foreign national, and then shared that information,

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:20.560
That is probably receiving a thing of value, but it hasn't been entirely established

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:21.980
And there are a lot of nuances here,

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:25.700
Have you received a thing of value if you know about the thing, and approve of sharing it

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:27.200
but don't share it yourself?

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:30.380
Did you solicit a thing of value, if you set up a meeting with a foreign national,

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:33.020
In the hope they would provide something they didn't provide et cetera

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:33.620
Et cetera

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:37.760
So many people in Trump's campaign have past relationships with Putin's regime

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:41.560
Trump's one time campaign manager, Paul Manafort, received around $30 million dollars

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:46.040
working for Oleg Deripaska, who is seen as one of Putin's closest business confidantes

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:49.640
Michael Caputo, an early Trump adviser had previously worked on a Russian campaign

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:51.780
to improve Putin's image among Americans

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:55.520
Trump's Attorney General, Jeff Sessions had to recuse himself from the Russia investigation

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:59.040
after  it was revealed he had previously undisclosed meetings with Sergei Kislyak

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:00.920
Russia's ambassador to the United States

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:04.500
And Michael Flynn, Trump's first national security advisor failed to disclose

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.560
payments that were made to him by an arm of the Russian government

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:11.540
And failure to disclose such payments is illegal for former military officers

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:17.160
Flynn also talks several times with Sergei Kislyak during the transition, the period after the election,

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:19.140
but before Trump became president.

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:21.320
One of those calls occurred on December the 29th,

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:25.000
which happened to be the same day the US announced new sanctions on Russia

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:27.760
Flynn initially denied that he discussed sanctions on that call,

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:30.660
but it became apparent that there was evidence to the contrary,

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:34.400
because Kislyak's calls are routinely monitored by US intelligence.

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:37.320
Discussing sanctions policy on that call was possibly illegal,

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:42.260
I mean Flynn was not yet the national security advisor, but he was trying to negotiate foreign policy

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:47.040
But a greater concern is that the day after Trump learned that had Flynn lied about discussion sanctions,

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:50.940
Trump asked FBI director James Comey for "loyalty"

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:53.140
And then, the day after Flynn eventually resigned,

00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:56.620
Trump asked Comey to drop the Flynn investigation altogether.

00:04:56.620 --> 00:05:00.660
This is alarming, because in the United States, the Justice Department and the FBI

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:03.460
are supposed to operate independently from the White House.

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:05.760
That's part of the reason why FBI directors serve a ten year term,

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:10.220
They're supposed to be above party politics and not beholden to one particular leader

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:14.500
Trump asking Comey to drop the Flynn investigation was a significant breach of protocol

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:17.620
In fact, some have argued that it amounts to that it amounts to obstruction of justice

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:20.900
which is the charge that Richard Nixon was eventually impeached for

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:23.500
And then a few months later, Trump fired James Comey

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:28.260
Initially the White House said the FBI Director was being fired for his mishandling of the Hillary Clinton email case

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:31.900
But then, after a few days of trying on trying on various justifications,

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:35.800
Trump acknowledged it was mostly about the collusion-with-Russia investigation

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:40.420
But if firing Comey was intended to make the investigation end, that gambit definitely failed

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:44.720
because a few days later, the Department of Justice appointed a Special Counsel, Robert Mueller

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:50.120
He is tasked with building a team to look into collusion and "any matters that arise from the investigation"

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:53.620
Since then, Mueller has been doing just that, and it has been reported that he's working with

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:58.020
at least two Grand Juries to decide who, if anyone, should be charged with crimes

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:03.620
The most serious evidence of collusion that has emerged so far involves a meeting with a Kremlin connected Russian lawyer

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:09.660
that was attended by Donald Trump Jr, Paul Manafort and Jared Kushner, the President's son in law and Senior Advisor

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:13.100
So during the setup for the meeting, Donald Trump Junior was told via email

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:16.980
That the lawyer had (quote) "incriminating information about Hillary Clinton"

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:22.180
that she wanted to share as (quote) "part of Russia and its government's support for Mr. Trump".

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:25.460
That should've been a HUGE red flag, like the correct response there is:

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:29.940
"I cannot accept anything of value from a foreign national in connection with a campaign -

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:34.280
- *ESPECIALLY* not if it's part of a foreign government's support for my candidate"

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:39.400
But instead Donald Trump Jr replied in part "if it's what you say, I love it, especially later in the summer"

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:46.060
The meeting that ensued remains secret for over a year until July of 2017 when the New York Times published a report about it

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:50.060
Donald Trump Jr initially said the meeting was about adoption, but then a day later said that

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:52.760
The meeting had been about Hillary Clinton, but that the lawyer

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:54.740
"Had no meaningful information"

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:58.580
Even if that's true, some legal scholars still think the meeting itself was illegal

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:02.440
depending on your definition of
"soliciting" and "thing of value"

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:05.580
Kushner, meanwhile failed to mention this, and also several other meetings

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:08.560
with Russian officials on his initial security clearance application

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:12.020
He also apparently asked the Russian government for a so called "back channel"

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:17.320
that would've allowed Kushner to communicate directly with Moscow without US intelligence listening in

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:22.560
Much of this is suspicious -  Trump Jr, Kushner, Manafort and Flynn have all been caught in lies

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:26.280
about the extent and nature with their contacts with the Russian government

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:31.880
But, [1] None of it is unambiguous evidence of collusion or cooperation with foreign nationals

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:35.580
And [2], None of what is known publicly involves the President himself

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:38.540
Except for him asking Comey to drop the investigation

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:42.060
That said, Trump's behavior to Russia has at times been, strange

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:45.980
As President, he had a private meeting with Putin without a US interpreter present

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:48.040
which is *extremely* unusual

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:50.120
Trump has also gone to great lengths not to criticize Putin

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:52.860
and he has refused to publicly acknowledge or condemn

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:54.920
the Putin regime's interference in our election.

00:07:55.220 --> 00:08:02.220
But none of that is illegal - and connecting the dots can lead to a very misleading picture when you don't have many dots.

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:06.740
What we know is that the Russian government sought to influence the US Presidential Election in Trump's favor,

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:09.960
That at least one person in Trump's campaign knew about this, and that

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:14.020
Several people close to Trump had interactions with Russian foreign nationals that they either

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:15.920
failed to disclose, or lied about

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:18.840
None of the meetings reported so far involve the President himself,

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:21.720
although he did try to interfere with the ensuring investigation

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:22.860
So that's what we know,

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:25.500
What we don't know, is almost everything.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:27.240
We don't know what was discussed in the meetings,

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:30.800
whether any American accepted any thing of value from a foreign national

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:36.080
And we certainly don't know if any American participated in collusion with the Russian government's efforts

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:39.640
Also we don't even know for sure what a 'thing of value' is

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:41.920
I think it's important to follow this story because it has big implications

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:46.680
for the present and future of our democracy but I also think it's really important to remember that we

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:51.140
are living in the middle of this story and we don't know how it's going to end

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:54.440
Now if you're looking for information to confirm your  preexisting biases

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:58.900
You'll find plenty of that on both sides but the whole truth, we just don't know that yet

00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:00.280
I hope we will some day.

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:03.460
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

